@database "memory"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: memory.guide 1.0 (29.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Memory"
@{h1}Memory

Notes an agent keeps for later: a fact it learned, a decision, a
preference you told it. Each memory has a title, a text, keywords and an
importance from 1 to 10, and belongs to the chat session it was stored in.
The agent finds them again by id or by meaning -- "how do we deploy?"
finds a note titled "Deploy checklist". You see and search them in the
@{b}Memory@{ub} panel; an optional hook reminds the model which memories it
has.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Memory panel with the memories of a session"}

@{code}
For users
  @{"  The Memory panel  " link panel}

For agents and developers
  @{"  The memory tool  " link tool}
  @{"  The memory hook  " link hook}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node panel "The Memory panel"
@{h1}The Memory panel

Open it from the launcher (@{b}Context@{ub}, "Memory"), or from a session's
info button, which opens it on that session.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Memory panel"}

@{h2}Which memories it shows

The panel follows the chat: it shows the memories of the session open
there and switches with it. Opened from a session's info button it stays
on that session -- the toolbar says so, and @{b}Follow the chat@{ub} lets go
of it. With no session open it says so.

@{h2}What you see

@{bullet}Three figures: how many memories, their average importance, and
how often agents have recalled them.
@{bullet}The 100 most recently used memories with title, the start of the
text, id, keywords, importance, accesses and last access. A session with
more says how many there are.

@{h2}What you can do

@{bullet}@{b}Search@{ub} finds memories by meaning and lists the 20
closest with how well they match. Switching sessions drops the search.
The list reloads every 5 seconds (the menu next to the reload button
changes that); a search stays and only the figures are brought up to
date, the reload button runs it again.
@{bullet}A row -- click, Enter or Space -- opens the memory with all its
text. @{b}Delete@{ub} there removes it, after asking.

Looking at or searching memories in the panel counts as no access: the
figures and the order of the list stay the agents' own.
@endnode

@node tool "The memory tool"
@{h1}The memory tool

The plugin gives an agent one tool, named like the plugin instance
(@{tt}memory@{utt} in the default setup). Every call names an
@{tt}operation@{utt}. The memories belong to the session the agent runs
in; ids count per session: @{tt}mem_001@{utt}, @{tt}mem_002@{utt}, ...
An id is never handed out twice, also after a delete.

@{h2}Operations

@{table}
operation | Needs | Does
store | title, content | saves a new memory
recall | memory_id | the whole memory; counts as an access
search | query | the memories closest in meaning, with a similarity
list | -- | memories with their text, 50 at a time
update | memory_id | changes the fields given, leaves the rest
delete | memory_id | removes the memory
@{body}

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Used by | Meaning
title | store, update | up to 200 characters
content | store, update | up to 5000 characters; the text the search compares
keywords | store, update | up to 20; left out on store, up to ten are taken from title and text
importance | store, update | 1 to 10; a store without it is 5
tags | store, update | free labels, returned by recall
memory_id | recall, update, delete | an id an earlier answer returned
query | search | what to look for, in words
n_results | search | 1 to 20; without it the server's search_n_results (5)
limit, offset | list | a page: up to 100, without limit 50
sort_by | list | accessed (default), created, updated or importance
sort_order | list | desc (default) or asc
@{body}

@{h2}Answers

@{bullet}store: the new @{tt}memory_id@{utt}, title, keywords and time.
@{bullet}recall: the whole memory, with @{tt}access_count@{utt} and
@{tt}accessed_at@{utt} counted up; nothing else changes.
@{bullet}search: @{tt}results@{utt} with id, title, text, similarity,
importance, keywords and accesses, the closest first; a search counts as
no access. The similarity is 1.0 for the same meaning; unrelated text
still scores about 0.5, so compare results with each other rather than
with a fixed number.
@{bullet}list: @{tt}memories@{utt}, @{tt}total@{utt}, @{tt}count@{utt}.
@{bullet}update: @{tt}updated_fields@{utt}; delete: @{tt}deleted: true@{utt}.
@{bullet}A refusal is @{tt}{"error": "..."}@{utt} saying what is missing
or which value is out of range (an importance of 11, a title over 200
characters); nothing is kept then.
An id that does not exist also brings @{tt}available@{utt}: the 15 most
recently used memories with id and title, so the agent can pick the right
one without another call. Only recall adds a @{tt}did_you_mean@{utt} --
update and delete never guess, so no memory is changed or lost by a
guess.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"operation": "store", "title": "Deploy checklist", "content": "Run the migrations before restarting the service.", "importance": 7}
{"operation": "search", "query": "how do we roll out a release?"}
{"operation": "recall", "memory_id": "mem_001"}
{"operation": "update", "memory_id": "mem_001", "importance": 9}
@{body}

@{h2}When a session is full

A session keeps at most @{tt}max_memories_per_session@{utt} memories
(the shipped configuration sets 1000, the plugin's own default is 5000,
0 means no limit). A store beyond that removes the least important one
first -- the oldest of those -- to make room.
@endnode

@node hook "The memory hook"
@{h1}The memory hook

@{tt}inject_memory_context@{utt} runs before each LLM call and tells the
model which memories it has, so it thinks of recalling them. It is off
until an agent switches it on.

@{h2}What the model gets

A message at the end of the history, from the plugin, with the id and
title of each chosen memory (titles cut at 80 characters) -- the text
itself stays out until the model recalls it:

@{code markdown}
## AVAILABLE MEMORIES

Use `memory(operation='recall', memory_id='...')` to access full content:

- `mem_001`: Deploy checklist
- `mem_004`: Python type hints
@{body}

Which memories: with semantic injection (the default) the ones closest in
meaning to the last message a person wrote; without it -- or when there
is no such message yet, or the search fails -- the most important, the
most recently used first among equals. A session without memories gets nothing. The message is
added only when it differs from the last one the hook added, so the
cached prompt in front of it stays as it was.

@{h2}Switching it on

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  hooks:
    enabled: true
    overrides:
      memory.inject_memory_context:
        enabled: true
        max_memories: 5
@{body}

@{h2}Settings

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
max_memories | 10 | how many memories the message names
use_semantic_injection | true | false: by importance and last use, not by meaning
@{body}

Both are read from the plugin's server entry; an agent's override, as
above, wins for that agent.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tool

The tool description and every parameter's description come from the
plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}. A refused call answers
@{tt}{"error": "Missing required parameter 'title' for store
operation"}@{utt}, @{tt}{"error": "Cannot update mem_001: importance:
Input should be less than or equal to 10"}@{utt} and the like. In the
chat, the call's status line ends with its result: "Stored: Deploy
checklist (mem_004)", "Recalled: Deploy checklist", "Updated mem_001:
importance".

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Tool answers are ordinary tool results: appended, nothing earlier
changes. @{tt}list@{utt} and @{tt}search@{utt} return each memory's
whole text -- up to 5000 characters each; a small @{tt}limit@{utt} or
@{tt}n_results@{utt} keeps an answer short. The hook appends only ids and
titles, at the end of the history, and only when they changed; it never
rewrites the system prompt or an earlier message.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}Memories belong to one session. A new chat starts without the
memories of the last one.
@{bullet}Tags are stored and returned by recall, but neither list nor
search can filter by them.
@{bullet}The search compares the text: a title changed by update is shown
at once, but only a new text changes what a search finds.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is on in the default configuration:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    memory:
      type: memory
      enabled: true
      max_memories: 10
      max_memories_per_session: 1000
@{body}

An agent sees the tool when its allowlist names it:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+memory/*"
@{body}

The plugin needs a vector store: @{tt}chromadb@{utt} (recommended), or
@{tt}sqlite-vec@{utt} with @{tt}sentence-transformers@{utt}; without one
it does not start. The embeddings are made locally with
all-MiniLM-L6-v2, which is downloaded once on first use; no memory text
leaves the machine.

@{h2}Server settings

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
storage_path | memories in the data directory | where memories are kept
max_memories_per_session | 5000 | the most a session keeps, 0 no limit; see "When a session is full"
auto_extract_keywords | true | false: a memory stored without keywords keeps none
search_n_results | 5 | results of a search that names no n_results
max_memories | 10 | the hook: how many memories it names
use_semantic_injection | true | the hook: chosen by meaning, not by importance
@{body}

Each session's memories are one JSON file, named after the session, in the
storage folder; the search index lies beside it in @{tt}vectors/@{utt}.
@endnode
